 "requested_at": "2026-11-12T19:52:14.452142Z",
 "text": "The clause is **not enforceable**. Section 106 of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* prohibits a landlord from directly or indirectly requiring a tenant to pay any amount other than rent for the rental unit, except as the Act permits. Nothing in the Act allows a landlord to demand a new rent deposit during the tenancy as a penalty for late rent. Section 4 also voids any term of a tenancy agreement that conflicts with the Act, so the landlord cannot rely on the clause even though the tenant agreed to it.\n\nLate payment has its own remedies under the Act, such as an N8 notice of termination for persistent late payment, but those are not a deposit demand.\n\nI cite these sections from memory, so check the current wording of s. 106 on e-Laws before relying on it.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 106",
